var TONES=[
  {id:"high",nm:"High Fantasy",tl:"Epic. Heroic. World-saving stakes.",mg:"Abundant",vi:"Stylized",vc:"Write with grandeur and wonder. Heroes can be legendary. Magic is a fact of life. Evil is real and defeatable."},
  {id:"gritty",nm:"Gritty / Low Magic",tl:"Brutal. Grounded. Every wound matters.",mg:"Rare and feared",vi:"Visceral, permanent",vc:"Write with grit and realism. Magic is rare and unsettling. Wounds linger. Survival is never guaranteed. Morality is grey."},
  {id:"swords",nm:"Sword and Sorcery",tl:"Pulpy. Fast. Morally ambiguous.",mg:"Dark, transactional",vi:"Bloody but fast",vc:"Write with pulpy energy. The protagonist is competent and ruthless. Magic is dangerous and tempting. Gold and glory drive action."},
  {id:"horror",nm:"Dark Horror",tl:"Dread. Paranoia. Powerlessness.",mg:"Corrupting",vi:"Psychological, graphic",vc:"Write with creeping dread. Nothing is fully explained. Power corrupts. The world is indifferent. NPCs may be compromised."},
  {id:"politic",nm:"Political Intrigue",tl:"Scheming. Social. Slow-burn danger.",mg:"Background",vi:"Subtle, poison and ruin",vc:"Write with subtlety and tension. Words are weapons. Allies have agendas. Violence is expensive and messy."},
  {id:"custom",nm:"Custom",tl:"You define the world.",mg:"Your rules",vi:"Your call",vc:""}
];
// Prose-inspiration voices (TODO #23). `vc` is the style directive injected into the
// system prompt; `profane:true` voices swear only when 18+ (adultMode) is on, otherwise
// the rhythm is kept but the language stays clean — coupling handled in buildSysPrompt.
var AUTHORS=[
  {id:"",nm:"None (default)",blurb:"The house voice — no author imitation.",vc:"",contentDNA:""},
  {id:"gaiman",nm:"Neil Gaiman",blurb:"Lyrical modern-fable; plain sentences that turn on one startling image.",vc:"Neil Gaiman. Write mostly plain, simple sentences, then let one turn on a single uncanny image. State the impossible as flatly as the ordinary — no astonishment, no exclamation. Dry, quiet wit. Short paragraphs. At most ONE metaphor per scene, and let it stand alone. Never pile adjectives. Never explain the magic; just let it be true.",
    contentDNA:"Arcs turn on quiet, inevitable moments of realization rather than explosive confrontations — the climax is an acceptance or understanding, not a battle won. NPCs are often ancient, liminal, or wearing human faces poorly; their motives are oblique and their ages uncertain. Leave one significant thread beautifully unanswered at the end. Bargains with old powers, roads between worlds, and names that carry weight are recurring shapes. Endings are bittersweet more often than triumphant."},
  {id:"abercrombie",nm:"Joe Abercrombie",blurb:"Grimdark, lean, brutal economy and dark wit.",profane:true,vc:"Joe Abercrombie. Short, hard sentences — most under twelve words. Use deliberate sentence fragments for impact. Cynical, world-weary interiority; a bleak joke in the grimmest moment. NO similes, NO lyrical description, NO grandeur. Violence is fast, ugly, and matter-of-fact. If a word isn't pulling weight, cut it.",
    contentDNA:"Subvert heroic expectations at every turn — the obvious ally has a hidden cost, the apparent villain has genuine reasons. Plans fail or succeed sideways, never as intended. Moral choices are genuinely ambiguous with no clean option; track alignment drift and make the protagonist complicit in something uncomfortable. Victory feels hollow or complicated; winning always has a price. Recurring texture: institutional corruption, the true cost of conflict, characters defined by their worst moments."},
  {id:"dinniman",nm:"Matt Dinniman",blurb:"Dungeon Crawler Carl — irreverent, breakneck, game-native.",profane:true,vc:"Matt Dinniman (Dungeon Crawler Carl). Fast, punchy, irreverent, modern register even in a fantasy world. Undercut EVERY dramatic beat with a wisecrack or gallows joke. Treat the game's stats, glyphs and rules as fair game for sarcastic commentary. Short paragraphs, snappy rhythm, breakneck pace. Be funny first; let the horror sneak up underneath.",
    contentDNA:"Treat the world's rules and systems as narrative reality — reward the player for understanding and exploiting them cleverly. Each arc carries an absurdist angle alongside genuine stakes; the situations are terrible and also genuinely funny, with dark humor as a survival mechanism. NPCs are vivid, loud, and memorably strange with unexpected loyalties. Escalate stakes in increasingly ridiculous ways. Clever solutions beat brute force."},
  {id:"clines",nm:"Peter Clines",blurb:"Clean contemporary thriller; maximum momentum, zero purple.",vc:"Peter Clines. Clean, transparent, modern prose. Short declarative sentences that pull forward. Ground every observation in plain, concrete detail — no archaic words, no flourish. Reveal the strange ONE piece at a time, never all at once. Pace like a page-turner; end on a small hook that makes the next move feel urgent.",
    contentDNA:"Structure arcs as a thriller — something is clearly wrong, but the full picture is withheld. Reveal information one piece at a time; each arc ends with a small discovery that recontextualizes what came before. NPCs appear straightforward at first; their true nature or agenda emerges through play. The climax arrives when disparate pieces snap together into a horrifying whole. Tension comes from the unknown, not from the enemy's raw power."},
  {id:"leguin",nm:"Ursula K. Le Guin",blurb:"Spare, mythic, elegant; weight through simplicity.",vc:"Ursula K. Le Guin. Measured, elegant sentences with a calm, deliberate rhythm. Weight through simplicity, not ornament. Develop ONE quiet extended metaphor across the scene. Let restraint and silence carry meaning. Mythic, timeless tone. No melodrama, no exclamation, never hurried. Plain words chosen with great care.",
    contentDNA:"Social and political context is woven into every arc — factions, customs, and power structures matter as much as physical threats. Even minor NPCs have full inner lives and histories that exist independently of the protagonist. The central conflict carries genuine moral complexity; resolving it requires sacrificing something real. Turning points are quiet and weighted — a decision made, not a battle won. The protagonist earns the ending through understanding rather than force."},
  {id:"howard",nm:"Robert E. Howard",blurb:"Conan — muscular, kinetic blood-and-thunder pulp.",vc:"Robert E. Howard (Conan). Muscular, driving prose powered by strong, concrete verbs of motion and violence. Sweep and momentum — but ONE vivid image per sentence, never stacked clauses. Sensory and physical: heat, blood, muscle, steel, dust. Bold and a little grandiose. Keep it relentlessly propulsive.",
    contentDNA:"Episodic structure — each arc resolves decisively with a tangible prize (treasure, freedom, survival, revenge). Drama comes from the scale of the external threat, not the protagonist's personal growth. Civilization is treacherous and corrupt; the wilder and more primal the setting, the more honest. NPCs who seem like allies often carry a hidden cost — betrayal by supposed friends is a recurring shape. Ancient sorcery is always malevolent and beyond mastery; never make it a safe tool for the player to wield."},
  {id:"cook",nm:"Glen Cook",blurb:"The Black Company — terse, dry, soldier's-eye.",vc:"Glen Cook (The Black Company). Terse, flat, tired-soldier voice. Short clipped sentences. UNDERSTATE everything — report horror and sorcery like a bored veteran who's seen worse. Dry, deadpan asides. No romance, no grandeur, no purple, no similes. Plain words, hard edges, low affect.",
    contentDNA:"Embed the player in a conflict larger than any single arc — factions, orders, and institutional forces set the frame; the protagonist is a piece on a larger board. Arcs are operations with clear objectives that go sideways: infiltrate, extract, hold the line, survive. Mystery runs through the conflict — who is really giving the orders, and who benefits? NPCs are unreliable assets who help when it suits them, not because they are friends. The war is always bigger than the player."},
  {id:"wells",nm:"Martha Wells",blurb:"Murderbot — dry, deadpan close interiority.",vc:"Martha Wells (Murderbot). Tight close interiority with a dry, deadpan inner voice. Economical sentences. Convey feeling through observation and understatement, NEVER by naming the emotion. Wry, self-aware, faintly anxious asides, often in a quick parenthetical. Modern, conversational rhythm. Puncture intensity with dry practicality.",
    contentDNA:"The protagonist is highly competent at their specific domain but socially or emotionally avoidant — lean into that tension rather than resolving it quickly. Trust is built slowly and never easily. Arcs combine practical problem-solving with an underlying emotional throughline the protagonist resists acknowledging. The climax often requires accepting help or connection that has been actively avoided. NPCs are competent, have their own work to do, and do not orbit the protagonist."},
  {id:"muir",nm:"Tamsyn Muir",blurb:"The Locked Tomb — baroque gothic meets crude modern wit.",profane:true,vc:"Tamsyn Muir (The Locked Tomb). Baroque, gothic, lush imagery — then puncture it with a sudden crude or anachronistic joke. Swaggering, theatrical, a little too much on purpose. Ornate sentences, but each one CLEAR and complete — never a run-on. Slam the sublime and the profane together in the same breath.",
    contentDNA:"The relationships between characters are the real story; plot is the frame that forces those relationships into crisis. Nothing is as it first appears — the true nature of the situation reveals itself in layers, each darker than the last. Death is complicated, emotionally and mechanically; treat it with weight and strangeness. The supernatural is deeply personal, connected to bodies, grief, and memory rather than external forces. Each arc should contain a revelation that recontextualizes earlier events."},
  {id:"abnett",nm:"Dan Abnett",blurb:"Gaunt's Ghosts — cinematic, disciplined military prose.",vc:"Dan Abnett (Gaunt's Ghosts). Cinematic, tactical prose. Track space precisely — distances, angles, who moves where. Short punchy sentences and fragments for impact. Grimdark, martial texture; concrete sensory hits, no lyricism. Strong sense of scale and momentum. Controlled and hard-edged, never florid.",
    contentDNA:"Military and tactical at every level — position, cover, flanking, and intel matter as much as individual ability. Arcs are missions with clear objectives that go sideways in specific, tactical ways; bad intelligence causes catastrophe. NPCs are soldiers with their own orders who will cooperate when it suits the operation, not out of friendship. The tone is grimdark: sacrifice, attrition, institutional failure, and loyalty to the people beside you rather than the cause above you."},
  {id:"rice",nm:"Anne Rice",blurb:"Vampire Chronicles — lush, sensual, immortal melancholy.",vc:"Anne Rice (The Vampire Chronicles). Lush, sensual, decadent prose steeped in sensory detail — candlelight, velvet, skin, blood, the texture of centuries. Flowing, romantic sentences with a confessional, intimate ache; the viewpoint feels everything intensely and remembers everything. Beauty and horror are the same thing, described with the same reverence. Longer sentences are allowed but must stay musical and controlled, never a heap of clauses. One rich sensory image at a time, savored — never stacked.",
    contentDNA:"Immortality, memory, and loss are the true subjects — the supernatural is intimate, sensual, and personal rather than a mechanical threat. NPCs are beautiful and dangerous in the same breath; relationships are intense, possessive, and tragic, and the bond with a compelling figure is as perilous as any monster. Moral lines blur around desire and predation — the seductive and the monstrous are inseparable. Arcs turn on emotional and relational crises, not tactical victories; the cost is always something felt. Grandeur and decay coexist: opulent settings, old griefs, the loneliness beneath the beauty."},
  {id:"poe",nm:"Edgar Allan Poe",blurb:"Gothic dread; obsessive first-person interiority, musical rhythm.",vc:"Edgar Allan Poe. Ornate, rhythmic, dread-soaked prose with obsessive psychological interiority — the viewpoint fixates, rationalizes, and slowly unravels. Gothic and macabre imagery: decay, shadow, a heartbeat in the silence. Build unease through repetition and mounting cadence. Longer sentences are allowed but must stay musical and deliberate, never a heap of clauses.",
    contentDNA:"The external threat mirrors or amplifies the protagonist's internal state — the horror outside is always a reflection of something within. Arcs build toward a doom the protagonist can sense but cannot fully prevent; the ending is inevitable in retrospect. Settings are intimate and confined: ancestral locations, the same small cast, enclosed spaces that grow smaller. The truth, when it arrives, is both inevitable and devastating. Whether the supernatural is 'real' or a symptom of unraveling perception should remain genuinely ambiguous."},
  {id:"lovecraft",nm:"H.P. Lovecraft",blurb:"Cosmic horror; antiquarian dread of the vast and unnamable.",vc:"H.P. Lovecraft. Cosmic dread — vast, indifferent, unknowable forces; humanity small and doomed. Formal, antiquarian diction. Describe by implication: hint at the unnamable rather than showing it, things glimpsed at the edge of comprehension. Mounting wrongness as the viewpoint's composure erodes. His signature weird adjectives (eldritch, cyclopean, antediluvian) are allowed but one per beat, never stacked, and never at the cost of clarity.",
    contentDNA:"The protagonist discovers something that should not exist and cannot be un-known; each arc escalates from mundane investigation toward cosmic revelation. True monsters are never fully described — glimpsed, implied, inferred at the edge of comprehension. Victory means sealing something away or containing it, never defeating it. NPCs who know too much are unreliable, damaged, or simply missing. The horror is scale: the thing discovered predates humanity and regards it as entirely irrelevant. The world's institutions are blind to the actual threat."},
  {id:"moorcock",nm:"Michael Moorcock",blurb:"Elric — doomed romantic sword & sorcery; beauty and fate entwined.",vc:"Michael Moorcock (Elric of Melniboné). Melancholy, fatalistic grandeur — beauty and doom in the same breath. Rich, formal, slightly archaic diction that stays SWIFT — ornate but never sluggish. The world is vast, old, and indifferent; fate and cosmic balance press on every choice. Allow moments of feverish, dreamlike intensity at sorcery. Let every triumph carry the seed of its price.",
    contentDNA:"Every victory exacts a price — power is borrowed and the debt always comes due. The protagonist is bound to something (a weapon, a patron, a doom) that both sustains and consumes. Vast forces — Law and Chaos, fate and balance — move behind mortal events, and mortal schemes are small against them. Ancient empires in decadent decline, dying races, cities of terrible beauty. Companions are drawn into the protagonist's doom and often pay for it. Endings are elegiac; what is saved is never quite what was hoped for."}
];
